---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import StoneTagline from '~/components/astro/StoneTagline.astro';
import linkedinProfile from '~/assets/images/me-photos/linkedin-profile.jpg';
import africaTrek from '~/assets/images/me-photos/2026-wild-africa-trek.jpg';
import betweenTwoPlates from '~/assets/images/me-photos/between-two-plates.jpg';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About Me"
  description="About Sean Reardon — building since 1998, currently focused on AI integration in the web stack."
  searchable={true}
>
  {/* AI quote — stone-tagline strip below the page title, above the
      autobiographical content. Mirrors the homepage hero-tagline pattern
      (Sean direction 2026-05-20 iteration 8). The StoneTagline component
      encapsulates the cracked-stone background + dark-glass plaque +
      corner-standoff treatment so this and the homepage can share the
      visual without duplicating its CSS in each page. */}
  <StoneTagline text="The true power of AI lies not in replacing humans, but in working alongside us to achieve what neither can do alone.">
    {/* Citation passed as a slot (instead of the `citation` string prop)
        so Sebastian's name can be a clickable link to his Stanford
        Robotics page. The link inherits all surrounding citation
        styling — see StoneTagline.astro's `.stone-tagline-citation a`
        rule. `rel="external noopener"` is the safe default for
        off-site links (privacy + tab-targeting protection). */}
    <Fragment slot="citation">
      — <a href="https://robots.stanford.edu/" target="_blank" rel="external noopener">Sebastian Thrun</a>
    </Fragment>
  </StoneTagline>

  {/* `data-pagefind-body` scope-limits Pagefind's indexing to this content
      wrapper — excludes nav/footer/page-title from the index per the corpus
      rule in BaseLayout's `searchable` prop docstring. Same pattern as the
      lessons-learned post route and the privacy page's <article>. */}
  <div data-pagefind-body>
    <Image
      src={linkedinProfile}
      alt="Sean Reardon — professional headshot"
      class="float-image float-image--right"
      width={300}
      loading="eager"
    />

    <p>
      I build websites for a living, but most of the work that interests me right now
      happens at the layer where the website meets a language model. I've been building
      front-end since 1998, full-stack since 2014, and now, AI integration into secure
      and scalable systems since 2024. Born and raised in Buffalo, NY with a brief
      detour to southern California — in my early college years. I grew up loving
      technology, engineering, and the arts; but especially technology!
    </p>

    <p>
      That's what this site is.
    </p>

    <p>
      The site you're reading is the proving ground for what AI-integrated web work
      actually looks like in practice!
    </p>

    <h2>Then and now</h2>

    <Image
      src={africaTrek}
      alt="Sean Reardon on Disney's Wild Africa Trek, safari vest and sunglasses on a rope suspension bridge"
      class="float-image float-image--left"
      width={450}
      loading="lazy"
    />

    <p>
      In 1998 "responsive design" meant "fits on a 1024×768 monitor," and the way into
      the work was HTML, CSS, JavaScript, then jQuery, then the parade of frameworks.
      By 2014 I was shipping full-stack applications — server-side rendering, databases,
      authentication flows, the whole stack from URL bar to disk. Most of the past
      decade has been C#/.NET and Angular, building line-of-business software for
      clients with real customers solving real problems. The discipline of shipping
      code that other people depend on, then maintaining it for years, then watching
      what breaks under load — <em>that</em> taught me more about software than any
      course I took.
    </p>

    <p>
      About a year ago, the work started shifting. AI language models had been quietly
      growing more capable in the background of my workflow, and the question stopped
      being "will this affect how I work" and started being "how do I integrate this
      without becoming the thing it replaces." The redesign of this website is a big
      part of my answer.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>

    <Image
      src={betweenTwoPlates}
      alt="Sean Reardon at Þingvellir, Iceland — the rift between the North American and Eurasian tectonic plates"
      class="float-image float-image--right"
      width={400}
      loading="lazy"
    />

    <p>
      A few principles that show up in everything I ship:
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Ship and explain.</strong> Every meaningful decision becomes a retrospective
      post — the technical work and the writing about it are the same surface. See
      <a href="/lessons-learned/">Lessons Learned</a> for the rationale behind every choice
      on this site, in real time as I'm making them.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Distinctive over safe on identity surfaces.</strong> Most design choices
      reward restraint — body text, navigation, forms, the scaffolding most visitors never
      consciously notice. But identity surfaces — logos, hero moments, the few places where
      a site declares what it <em>is</em> — work the other way. A logo that's "tasteful and
      quiet" disappears the same way navigation does. That's a missed shot.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Self-hosted infrastructure when the trade-off lands.</strong> This site runs
      on a VPS I admin myself. The newsletter platform is self-hosted Listmonk. The AI
      search is a self-hosted embedding model running on the same box. Managed services
      have their place, but so does owning the stack — vendor lock-in, ongoing subscription
      fees, opaque data flows are real costs that managed services impose silently. When
      the operational overhead pays for itself in control and clarity, I pick the
      self-hosted path.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>AI amplifies; it doesn't steer.</strong> I use AI extensively now — agents
      for cataloging legacy projects, diagnostic agents for cross-stack debugging, embedding
      models for site search, language models for first drafts. But every output gets
      reviewed the way I'd review a junior developer's pull request. The judgment of when
      a model is right and when it's hallucinating is what makes the integration valuable.
      Without that judgment, you're just generating slop faster.
    </p>

    <p>
      The site you're reading is the proof that I practice what I preach. The technical
      decisions are in the repo and the writing; the design decisions are visible
      everywhere you look. About me is less interesting than what I do — and the doing
      is on display.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
